# The breaks the flat syntax adds - catalogued here with the other deliberate breaks.
'Flattening: depth moves into the key'
description: the syntax space flat rows claim, and what it used to mean
- >
  [Flattening](*::language:flattening) widens the deliberate distance from YAML. The breaks it
  adds:
- !!<*yamlover: $defs: bullets>
  - '**`a: b: c` becomes structure.** YAML rejects the line outright ("mapped values are not allowed in this context") - so no valid YAML document changes meaning; a class of YAML *errors* becomes meaningful yamlover. What does change is yamlover''s own former tolerance: the bare spacey-colon scalar (`a: b: c` read as the string `b: c`) is re-read as a nested path. The string reading needs its quotes - `a: ''b: c''` - which is exactly the spelling the serializer has always emitted for such values, so canonical documents are untouched; only hand-typed, never-rewritten files can be affected.'
  - '**`-` is a path segment, not a key.** In YAML `-: x` is a mapping with the literal key `-`. yamlover already reads the standalone `-: x` as a keyless element (the tolerated spelling of `- x`); flattening extends `-` into paths - trailing appends, middle addresses the last keyless member. A literal `-` key must be quoted: `''-'': x`.'
  - '**Depth without indentation.** In YAML, nesting depth IS indentation. A flat row spells depth in the key chain instead, and only a continuation block returns to columns - computed as if the row were unfolded. Two documents with identical indentation profiles can now differ in depth.'
